PUBLIC RESPONSE: F. None of the above

All five of the forms are used by Chaucer as end-rhyme words in the Duchess and each of them is regularly paired.

* wente is the ordinary past of wend(en) and e.g. is set in couplet rhyme with sente/saute etc. in the octosyllabic couplets of the dream vision;
* here is a normal rhyme-word for the MS and is rhymed in the poem with dere/ere/mere and the like;
* fool is a normal rhyme-word, rhymed with cool/dool etc.;
* hool < OE hol ‘whole’ is the Middle English rhyme partner of fool/cool and is used in the same rhyme family in the Duchess;
* countour / countoer, ‘counter, outline, circuit’, is an end-word in the poem and is rhymed with tour/recountour/withoutour etc.

Hence each of the items A–E actually occurs as a rhyming end-word in The Book of the Duchess, so no one of them is a non-rhyming form in the work.

